  What I found in the repo:

  - [1mTwo disjoint runtimes.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a browser login form. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m is a Node entry
    point ([38;5;153mpackage.json[39m [38;5;153mmain[39m) that prints a greeting. They share no code and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m isn't referenced by [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.
  - [1mNo dependencies at all[0m — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mdependencies[39m, no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no test runner.
  - [1mA live concern:[0m [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m logs a username at login and [38;5;153mapp.js:24[39m logs the whole result object. Any logging subsystem
    that reaches production has to answer "what must never be recorded" before it ships, since this flow handles
    passwords.

  First question — everything else depends on it.

  The fork is [1mwhich runtime the logging subsystem has to serve[0m, because it decides where logs can physically go. Browser
  code can't write a log file; it either stays in the user's devtools console (invisible to you in production) or gets
  shipped over the network to a collector you run — which means a transport, batching, failure handling, and a privacy
  review. Node code can write to stdout/file cheaply and a log aggregator picks it up, with none of that machinery.
  Building for both means either one abstraction with two backends (more design, one vocabulary) or two small loggers
  (less design, duplicated conventions). This choice is expensive to reverse: the transport decision shapes the logger's
  API, so picking "browser" later after building a Node-only logger means redoing the interface.

  My recommendation is the browser login flow — that's where the code that actually smells like a production issue lives
  (auth, validation, a form users submit), whereas [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a greeting stub with nothing to debug.
